Addressing these gaps, we introduce Multi-Spotlight Domain-Adaptive Collaborative Filtering (MSDCF), a novel multi-task learning architecture for multi-sparse cross-recommendations. This framework enables mutual interaction between multiple sparse domains by constructing two complementary domains with shared data that are rich in domain-specific characteristics. Our model achieves this through three core modules: first, providing targeted functions to capture unique individual preferences in each domain; second, employing a Spotlight separation mechanism using two shared components, respectively for domain-unique and domain-specific specificities; third, utilizing gradient isolation for efficient training and optimization;

In addition, we demonstrate the theoretical effectiveness of our approach through gradient separation and develop a comprehensive multi-task learning architecture for cross-recommendation. Our comprehensive experimental evaluation, conducted on two real-world datasets, confirms that the proposed MSDCF substantially improves recommendation precision compared to existing state-of-the-art methods.

